@* The portal's sign-out control, wherever it appears (issue #43). One click
   signs out, with no interstitial - but still by posting, which is the part
   that matters and the part the deleted confirm screen actually existed for.

   A bare GET endpoint is signed out by anything that merely fetches the URL -
   a link preview, a prefetching browser - so this is a real form submit
   carrying an antiforgery token, and AppRoutes.Logout answers POST only.

   Deliberately *not* a Blazor named form, unlike every other form in the
   portal. A named form posts back to the page it is rendered on and is
   dispatched by the component that declared it, and this control is rendered
   in the chrome: its page is whichever one the User happens to be looking at.
   Posting to one endpoint instead keeps the sign-out in one place and keeps
   it out of a circuit - SignOutAsync writes to the response, which needs an
   HttpContext an interactive render does not have (ADR 0003). *@

<form class="hub-signout" method="post" action="@AppRoutes.Logout">
    <AntiforgeryToken />

    <button type="submit" class="@Class"
            title="@Constants.Logout.ControlTitle" aria-label="@Constants.Logout.ActionLabel">
        @ChildContent
    </button>
</form>

@code {
    // The look of the button, which is the call site's business: the nav wants
    // a round icon button on the gradient band, and a login card wants the
    // same understated link its other way-out wears.
    [Parameter]
    public string Class { get; set; } = "";

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
